//? Title: Fit a linear model by generalized least squares
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Fit with a supplied residual covariance

The same instrument yields two measurements of a common level. Its
calibration gives the first measurement a variance of 1, the second a
variance of 3, and their covariance as 0.5. Generalized least squares
uses those quantities to estimate the level and its uncertainty. The
design has one column of ones because the level is the only coefficient.

```{cpp, id=known-gls, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/gls.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using datamunge::linalg::DenseMatrix;
    const DenseMatrix<double> design(2, 1, 1);
    const DenseMatrix<double> residual_covariance(2, 2, {1, .5, .5, 3});
    const auto fit = datamunge::stats::generalized_least_squares(design, {1, 3}, residual_covariance);
    std::println("| Estimated level | Standard error |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: |");
    std::println("| {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", fit.coefficients[0], std::sqrt(fit.covariance(0, 0)));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Estimated level | Standard error |
| ---: | ---: |
| 1.333 | 0.957 |
// result_end

The first reading has the smaller variance and receives more weight.
The standard error uses the covariance supplied by calibration.

> Estimate correlation within independent blocks

Three instruments each yield four consecutive readings from a linear
trend. The fit estimates the trend coefficients, the marginal residual
variance, and the within-instrument correlation by maximum likelihood.
AR(1) assigns correlation $\rho^k$ to readings $k$ time steps apart.
Compound symmetry assigns $\rho$ to every pair from the same instrument.
The groups identify instruments, and the row order within each group
defines the AR(1) time steps.

```{cpp, id=structured-gls, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/gls.hpp>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    datamunge::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> design(12, 2, 1);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < design.rows(); ++i)
        design(i, 1) = static_cast<double>(i % 4);
    const std::vector<double> readings{4, 5.2, 7.4, 7.7, 4.1, 6.8, 6.9, 8.6, 1.4, 2.2, 4.6, 6.2};
    const StructuredGlsOptions options{.groups = {10, 10, 10, 10, 20, 20, 20, 20, 30, 30, 30, 30}};
    const auto ar = structured_gls(design, readings, GlsCorrelation::AR1, options);
    const auto cs = structured_gls(design, readings, GlsCorrelation::CompoundSymmetry, options);
    if (!ar.converged || !cs.converged || ar.at_boundary || cs.at_boundary)
        throw std::runtime_error("Expected two interior likelihood fits");

    std::println("| Residual correlation model | Slope | Correlation | Marginal variance | Log likelihood |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    const auto print = [](const char* name, const StructuredGlsResult& result) {
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", name,
                     result.fit.coefficients[1], result.correlation, result.residual_variance,
                     result.fit.log_likelihood);
    };
    print("AR(1)", ar);
    print("Compound symmetry", cs);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Residual correlation model | Slope | Correlation | Marginal variance | Log likelihood |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| AR(1) | 1.445 | 0.781 | 1.641 | -15.763 |
| Compound symmetry | 1.457 | 0.851 | 1.926 | -14.289 |
// result_end

Compound symmetry gives the higher likelihood for these readings.
Both fits estimate a positive correlation and give similar slopes.

[Pinheiro and Bates (2000)|https://doi.org/10.1007/b98882] ·
[R nlme reference|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/nlme/html/gls.html]
